\begin_layout Section
Background
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 1999, we created a program, Mega2 [], to aid us in our linkage studies.
 We found it necessary to analyze the data with several different programs;
 it was often difficult to get data into the format required by each different
 linkage computer program.
 Not only must the data be converted to the proper format, but also the
 loci must be reordered into their proper order.
 Writing custom reformatting scripts can be error-prone and time-consuming.
 We created the Mega2 program to facilitate these conversions; the initial
 Mega2 could read input data in LINKAGE format or Mega2 table format and
 convert to a few standard package like Loki [], Mendel [], and Merlin [].
 In addition, Mega2 allowed trait locus penetrance data, allele frequencies
 data, etc.
 to be added and used by the linkage programs.
 Necessary steps, like calculating the allele frequencies if necessary and
 not provided, or cleaning the data for HWE consistency can be requested.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As time went on, other linkage analysis programs where added to the set
 of programs that Mega2 could massage data for.
 Now, there are over 40 programs supported among them: SOLAR, PLINK, SimWalk2,
 Cranefoot, IQLS, FBAT, MORGAN, BEAGLE, Eigenstrat, Structure, and PLINK/SEQ.
 In addition, several new inputs are supported such as PLINK [], VCF/BCF
 [], and IMPUTE/BGEN [] formats are processed.
 The new data input formats were needed to represent additional data as
 well as more data than were available just a few years earlier.
 To cope with the additional data, both contents and processing time, Mega2
 was enhanced to store genotype data in compressed format (as is commonly
 done) and the results of data cleaning and allele frequency calculation
 was stored in a canonical format so that different linkage analysis programs
 can use these data without recalculating them for each use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The canonical representation for the pedigree, genotype and phenotype data
 is stored in a SQLite3 database[].
 Processing libraries for SQLite3 databases are available in several popular
 computer languages.
 In our case, many programs for linkage analysis are available in R [] but
 they require the genetic data to be converted to R data frame format.
 We created an R package, Mega2R [], available via CRAN [], which can convert
 the canonical data stored in SQLite3 into R data frames.
 The package also provides data to decompress genotype data and iterate
 over ranges of markers and genes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unfortunately, with all the GWAS data generated in a study especially if
 imputation [] is performed, Mega2 is faced again with too much genotype
 data for it to process.
 But fortunately, the new genotype data is generated separately for each
 chromosome; in addition, most analyses can only be done one chromosome
 at a time.
 Mega2 can take advantage of this orthogonality and generate and subsequently
 process a distinct SQLite3 database for each chromosome.
 And it can process each chromosome's data at the same time on a sufficiently
 powerful multiprocessor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Implementation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Working Directory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Mega2 can process a set of input files that contains a genotype file
 with multiple chromosomes as it always has.
 The full explanation is in the Mega2 manual.
 But the same Mega2, given select command line flags, can process files
 of chromosomes, for an arbitrary list of chromosomes and on multiple machines.
 The first thing to note is that Mega2 must be careful about which directories
 to store data in.
 For example, suppose we were computing Merlin and Mendel results on the
 same set of chromosomes at the same time (different machines).
 It would be very easy to have the MEGA2.LOG (and related files) be written
 to the same directory and have one an unusable mess.
 If the MEGA2.LOG (etc) file was stored in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

summary
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 directory (named by the date: year-month-date-hour-minute), it is till
 possible that two different computers might start a task at exactly the
 same time.
 Most of the data files for Merlin and Mendel are named differently and
 would likely not be blended; this can not be guaranteed in general.
 So the first necessary solution step is to generate a working directory
 that at least had the chromosome number at part of its name.
 It would also be useful to add a component with the name of the analysis
 that could also serve as the name of the processor job.
 Thus we can easily separate the Mendel job from the Merlin job.
 Finally, Mega2 has always supported an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Output_Directory
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 field to indicate where relative to the current directory the generated
 results should be stored.
 Often this value is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which means that the results should be in the current directory.
 But this field could be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Mendel
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for the Mendel jobs and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Merlin
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for the Merlin jobs.
 The lesson from all this is the data are stored in the the directory constructe
d below relative to the current directory:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Job_Name/Output_Directory/chrxx
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Output_Directory can be empty.
 But Job_Name and the chromosome name (chrxx) must be present.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
MEGA2.BATCH file
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It would be rather inconvenient to enter the same (or similar) data 23 times
 to create processing jobs for all the chromosomes.
 We will take advantage of the MEGA2.BATCH file to simplify and standardize
 multi chromosome operation.
 Firstly, you should find a MEGA2.BATCH file that you have used before for
 the kind of analysis you plan to do.
 Alternatively, you can manually start an interactive Mega2 run which will
 as a side affect create a MEGA2.BATCH file.
 (Note: you might process chromosome 21 or 22 for this task; they tend to
 have the fewest markers.) Now when you are supplying chromosome arguments
 (will show you in just a bit) you can follow the MEGA2.BATCH file on the
 command line with more arguments, viz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
mega2 <options> <chromosome options> MEGA2.BATCH arg1 arg2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the MEGA2.BATCH file is processed each line is scanned for a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

$
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

%
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 followed by a digit (e.g.
 $1, $2, %2); if one is found, the two characters are replaced by the indicated
 argument following MEGA2.BATCH on the command line.
 Suppose the MEGA2.BATCH has a line that reads:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Output_Path=%2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This would be rendered in the file as
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Output_Path=arg2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

arg2
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 being the corresponding text from the command line.
 Thus we might be able to use the exact same BATCH file for both Mendel
 and Merlin and have the output go to different directories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of course, this example is not the real reason for this mechanism; we want
 to indicate what chromosome we are processing and possibly where to find
 the SQLite3 database that corresponds to that chromosome.
 Our convention is that argument 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 contains the chromosome that this invocation of Mega2 is processing.
 Thus to set the chromosome for processing we would use the line
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Chromosome_Single=$0
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And to chose the database that contains the chromosome, we would use
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
DBfile_name=<mydir>/%1/db%0.db
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The %1 fetches the first argument after the BATCH argument from the
 command line, allowing me to organize all the database files into a single
 directory.
 I tend to use 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

db
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as the directory name or possibly 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

dbx
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: %0/$0 always expands to two characters (for chromosomes 1 to 22).
 In particular, if the chromosome is 7, then %0/$0 is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

07
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 – not 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

7
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 If you are have a file containing sample data, it may be named by just
 the chromosome number without the leading 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 In this case, you may use 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

+
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 vs 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as the second digit, viz.
 %+/$+ instead of the %0/$0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
<chromosome options>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Mega2 <chromosome options> is indicated by the keyword 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

- - chr
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (or -c) followed by a list or range of numbers, viz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
–chr 1,2,3-5
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-c 1-22
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first line indicates chromosome 1, 2, 3, 4, 5; the second line indicates
 all the autosomes.
\end_layout

